{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"07","chapter_title":"AI-Enhanced Presentation Development","title":"Accessibility Checks for Slides","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting accessibility checks for slides to presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development","accessibility","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/05/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_calibration","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_in_design_large_text_threshold","cat100-supplement_05_07_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_in_design_large_text_threshold","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-03":["cat100-supplement_05_07_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Accessibility Checks for Slides\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-07-accessibility-checks-for-slides.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Accessibility Checks for Slides: Accessibility, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n<youtube-embed id=\"PHOJwnSV6t4\" title=\"Universal Design for Learning and CAST\" caption=\"Watch before the slide audit. Use the accessibility frame to decide which fixes are part of the design, not just cosmetic cleanup.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n**Watch for:** Which slide problem is an access barrier, and which fix would remove it?\n**Use this here because:** The video shows why accessibility is a design requirement, which is the same standard you apply when you revise alt text, headings, color, and contrast.\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA slide that only some of your audience can read is a slide that failed for the rest. Low-contrast text disappears for anyone with reduced vision or on a sun-washed projector. A chart with no alternative text is silent to a screen-reader user. Status dots that mean \"red equals risk\" are invisible to the roughly one in twelve men with color vision deficiency (about 8% of men of Northern European descent; prevalence is lower in Asian and African populations). None of these problems show up when the designer, who can see the slide perfectly, looks at it; they show up only when you test against specific, measurable criteria.\n\nThis section teaches a concrete, repeatable accessibility pass built on the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: check contrast against the 4.5:1 ratio, give every meaningful image alternative text, and never let color carry meaning alone. The artifact is a storyboard that records each check and the fix it triggered, so a reviewer can confirm the slide works for varied viewers.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team is proud of a sleek slide: light gray headings on white, a clean chart, and green and red dots showing project status. In the room, a reviewer with color blindness asks which projects are at risk, and cannot tell, because to him the dots look identical. A second reviewer squints at the gray heading and gives up. The slide looked elegant to the designers and excluded two people in the first row. Accessibility is not a finishing touch; it is whether the slide reaches its audience at all.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAccessibility on slides is not subjective taste; it is a set of testable criteria, and three of them catch the large majority of real problems:\n\n- **Contrast.** Text must stand out enough from its background to be legible. [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) sets a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1 for normal text (3:1 for large text); these are the Level AA thresholds, the conformance level institutions typically target (WebAIM). The ratio is computed from the relative luminance of the two colors; you do not eyeball it, you check it. Light gray on white, a common \"elegant\" choice, often lands near 1.9:1, far below the line.\n- **Text alternatives.** Every image that carries meaning needs alt text that states the point, not the file type. \"Chart\" is useless; \"Bar chart; signups rose from 1,200 to 2,400 after launch\" conveys what a sighted viewer gets.\n- **Do not rely on color alone.** If color is the only thing distinguishing two states (green vs. red, used vs. free), viewers with color vision deficiency cannot read it. Add a label, a shape, or a pattern so the meaning survives in grayscale.\n\nThese map directly to the data-ink idea but extend it: an element must not only carry meaning, it must carry that meaning to *every* viewer in the room. A useful gut-check is the grayscale test: if you converted the slide to grayscale, would it still be readable and would every distinction still be clear? If contrast collapses or the status dots merge, the slide was relying on something not everyone can perceive.\n\nCurrent tooling (as of 2026) makes these checks cheap to run. PowerPoint ships a built-in Accessibility Checker (Review > Check Accessibility) that flags missing alt text and color-only issues, and on Copilot+ PCs (Microsoft 365 Version 2512 and later) Word and PowerPoint auto-generate alt-text suggestions on-device via the NPU, with a toggle at File > Options > Accessibility. Google Slides has no built-in checker and relies on add-ons such as Grackle Slides or Accessibility Checker for Slides. The free WebAIM Contrast Checker works standalone for any tool. Auto-generated alt text is a bounded AI move: verify it against what the chart actually shows before accepting it, and expect menu paths and build numbers to change.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: run a three-point accessibility check on one slide and fix what fails.\n\nStarting slide:\n\n```\nTitle color:  #BBBBBB on #FFFFFF background\nChart image:  no alt text\nStatus dots:  green = on track, red = at risk (no labels)\n```\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Contrast: #BBBBBB on white is about 1.9:1, below the 4.5:1 minimum for normal text. To get the number, open a contrast checker (for example, the WebAIM Contrast Checker), paste #BBBBBB into Foreground Color and #FFFFFF into Background Color, and read the ratio; the tool instantly reports the number and a Pass/Fail against Normal Text (AA, 4.5:1) and Large Text (AA, 3:1). In PowerPoint, the built-in Accessibility Checker (Review > Check Accessibility) flags low contrast and missing alt text as you author. Darken the title to #1A2A4F, which exceeds 4.5:1.\n2. Alt text: add a description that states the point, not just the type:\n\n   `Alt: \"Bar chart; signups rose from 1,200 to 2,400 after launch.\"`\n\n3. Color-only meaning: a colorblind viewer cannot tell the dots apart, so add a label to each:\n\n   `On track (green) / At risk (red)` with text labels next to the dots.\n\n4. Re-check all three. Now the slide passes contrast, has alt text, and does not rely on color alone.\n\nEach fix targets a different viewer. The contrast fix helps anyone with low vision or a bad projector; the alt text reaches screen-reader users; the labels reach colorblind viewers. The same \"elegant\" slide failed three different people in three different ways, which is why a single visual judgment (\"looks clean\") is not an accessibility check.\n\nWork one accessibility check at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run a three-point accessibility check on one slide\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The title is #BBBBBB on a white background and looks elegant. What do you do first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep it; light gray reads as refined\",\"Measure the contrast ratio; #BBBBBB on white is about 1.9:1, below the 4.5:1 minimum\",\"Make the font larger and leave the color\",\"Add a drop shadow to the text\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Measure, do not eyeball. 1.9:1 fails [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) for normal text. Darken the title to something like #1A2A4F, which clears 4.5:1. Contrast is a number you check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The chart has no alt text. What should the alt text say?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"\\\"chart\\\" or the file name like image1.png\",\"A description that states the point: \\\"Bar chart; signups rose from 1,200 to 2,400 after launch\\\"\",\"Nothing; sighted viewers can see it\",\"\\\"See chart on slide\\\"\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Alt text must deliver the same information a sighted viewer gets, not name the file type. \\\"Chart\\\" is useless to a screen-reader user.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Status dots use green for on track and red for at risk with no labels. Add a text label to each so meaning survives in grayscale, then re-check all three. Color must never be the only carrier of meaning.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A slide uses green dots for on track and red dots for at risk, with no labels. Why does this fail a colorblind viewer even though the colors look distinct to the designer?\" explanation=\"When color is the only thing distinguishing two states, a viewer with color vision deficiency sees the dots as identical. The meaning must be backed by a label, shape, or pattern so it survives in grayscale.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The dots are simply too small to see\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Color alone carries the meaning, so a colorblind viewer cannot tell the states apart\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Green and red always have enough contrast\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The slide needs a brighter theme\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTest contrast against 4.5:1, give every meaningful image alt text that states the point, and never let color be the only carrier of meaning. If a fix would help only some viewers and exclude others, it is not finished.\n\nCheck a slide against three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Can every viewer, including those with low vision or color blindness or using a screen reader, get the slide's point?\n2. **Evidence check:** What is the measured contrast ratio, what does the alt text say, and what label backs each color-coded element?\n3. **Revision check:** Which specific failure did you fix, and to which [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) criterion does it map?\n\nIf the slide depends on color, fine print, or an unlabeled image, it has not passed.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating accessibility as a stylistic preference (\"I like high contrast\") rather than a measurable threshold. 4.5:1 is a number you check, not a feeling; a slide either clears it or it does not.\n\nThe second is writing alt text that names the file instead of the meaning. \"image1.png\" or \"chart\" tells a screen-reader user nothing; the alt text must deliver the same information a sighted viewer takes from the visual.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the three checks to a slide with a photo and a caption overlaid on it, a common design that quietly fails. Contrast: white caption text over a busy photo can swing from readable to invisible depending on the pixels behind each letter, so you add a solid or darkened band behind the text to guarantee the ratio. Alt text: the photo either carries meaning (then describe it) or is decorative (then mark it as such so the screen reader skips it rather than announcing a filename). Color-only: if the caption uses a colored word to flag something, add a non-color cue.\n\nNow stress-test with the grayscale conversion. Drop the slide to grayscale and look again: the caption band keeps the text legible, the decorative photo is correctly skipped by assistive tech, and no meaning was hiding in a color that just turned gray. If the slide survives grayscale and a screen-reader pass, it will survive the actual room, including the viewers the designer cannot see the slide as.\n\nOrder the accessibility pass so each check depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Measure text contrast against the 4.5:1 minimum (3:1 for large text)\", \"Darken or recolor any text that falls below the ratio\", \"Give every meaningful image alt text that states the point, not the file type\", \"Find any meaning that rests on color alone\", \"Back each color-coded element with a label, shape, or pattern\", \"Run the grayscale test to confirm every distinction still reads\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each visual element; on the right, its contrast ratio, alt text, and whether any meaning rests on color alone. Flag every element that fails one of the three checks. By the end of 05.07, your note should show that the slide was tested, not just admired.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the measured contrast ratio of the text against its background?\n2. Does every meaningful image have alt text that states the point?\n3. Does any meaning rest on color alone, and how is it backed up?\n4. Would the slide survive a grayscale and screen-reader pass?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe storyboard is strong when a reviewer can see, for each slide, the contrast ratio, the alt text, and the non-color cue for any coded element, plus the fix each check triggered. Treat \"it looks clean to me\" as the start of the check, not the result.\n\nA useful note would sound like this: \"I changed the title from #BBBBBB (about 1.9:1) to #1A2A4F (above 4.5:1) to meet [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) contrast, added alt text stating the signup increase, and labeled the status dots so the meaning does not rely on green versus red. I rejected a suggestion to use a lighter, trendier title color, because it would have dropped contrast back below the minimum.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same content to a printed handout that may be photocopied in black and white. Two of the three checks transfer immediately and one gets harder: contrast still matters (and copies degrade it further), color-only meaning becomes fatal because the copy is grayscale by definition, and alt text has no role since there is no screen reader for paper. If your slide already passed the grayscale test, the handout works; if it relied on color, the photocopy will strip the meaning out entirely.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how contrast, alt text, and not-color-alone checks feed evidence-based revision of the storyboard:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"accessibility\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-07-accessibility-checks-for-slides-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of the 4.5:1 contrast rule, alt text, and color-only meaning tied to [WCAG](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/).\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a low-contrast color-coded slide with its accessible rebuild.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the three checks to a photo-with-caption slide.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the grayscale test exposed a hidden failure.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence for CAT 100 05.07. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_accessibility_checks_for_slides\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm text clears 4.5:1 contrast, every meaningful image has alt text that states the point, and no distinction rests on color alone.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"accessibility","concept_ids":["accessibility","cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"],"stem":"Which slide choice passes a basic accessibility check?","options":["Light gray (#BBBBBB) body text on a white (#FFFFFF) background.","Dark navy (#1A2A4F) text on white, alt text on every chart, and status shown by label plus color not color alone.","Red and green status dots with no labels.","A chart image with no alt text because it 'looks self-explanatory'."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong contrast, alt text, and non-color-only encoding together meet the core accessibility requirements.","difficulty":"easy","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_low_contrast","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which note gives BigAL a concrete accessibility fault to fix?","options":["Is this accessible?","My slide marks 'on track' green and 'at risk' red with only dots, so a colorblind viewer cannot tell them apart. How should I label them?","Make it accessible.","I think it is fine."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The note names a specific color-only encoding problem so feedback can target a labeling fix.","difficulty":"medium","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_color_only_meaning","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"What is a sound boundary for using AI on accessibility?","options":["Ask AI to declare the deck accessible and skip checking.","Ask AI to draft alt text for each chart, then verify each draft actually matches what the chart shows.","Let AI pick any color theme without checking contrast.","Avoid AI and also skip the manual contrast check."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can draft alt text, but you must verify it describes the real image and still confirm contrast yourself.","difficulty":"hard","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_no_alt_text","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A reviewer flags 'low contrast' on your title. Which revision is the real fix?","options":["Make the title font larger but keep it light gray.","Darken the title color until it reaches at least a 4.5:1 contrast ratio against the background.","Add an animation to the title.","Center the title."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Contrast is fixed by adjusting the color values to meet the ratio, not by resizing or animating the text.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_in_design_large_text_threshold","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your 40pt slide title is a light color and you want to verify its contrast. Which WCAG AA threshold applies?","options":["4.5:1, the same as body text.","3:1, the large-text threshold (text 18pt and up, or 14pt bold).","No threshold; large text is exempt from contrast checks.","7:1, because titles matter more."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"WCAG AA sets 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text, defined as 18pt and up (or 14pt bold). A 40pt title is large text, so 3:1 applies; that is a lower bar, not an exemption, and a pale title can still fail it. (7:1 is the AAA normal-text level, not the AA title rule.)","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_contrast_threshold_confusion","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_05_07_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-07-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_low_contrast","pattern":"color_contrast_ignored","description":"Choosing text and background colors that fail contrast requirements","trigger":"learner uses light gray text on a white slide, failing WCAG contrast","feedback":"Check text against background for at least a 4.5:1 contrast ratio for normal text; adjust the darker or lighter value.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_no_alt_text","pattern":"missing_alt_text","description":"Adding images and charts with no alternative text for screen readers","trigger":"learner inserts a chart image with no alt text describing the trend","feedback":"Write alt text that states what the image shows and its point, so a screen-reader user gets the same information.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_accessibility_checks_for_slides_color_only_meaning","pattern":"meaning_by_color_only","description":"Encoding meaning with color alone so colorblind viewers lose it","trigger":"learner marks 'pass' green and 'fail' red with no label or shape difference","feedback":"Pair color with a label, icon, or pattern so the meaning survives without color perception.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_7_contrast_threshold_confusion","pattern":"contrast_threshold_confusion","description":"Applying the 4.5:1 normal-text threshold to large display headings, or assuming a large title needs no contrast check at all.","trigger":"learner over-corrects a 40pt title against 4.5:1, or skips checking it because 'big text is always readable'","feedback":"Large text (18pt and up, or 14pt bold) uses the 3:1 threshold under WCAG AA. That is a lower bar, not no bar: a pale-gray 40pt title can still fail 3:1. Check the ratio with the right threshold for the text size.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"dd3636c7db55f558a430e88acd6561c590b26241d3078d09b98b9b0fcc2749c6","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}